cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Refusal Signal Extraction: Semantic Leakage through LLM Refusals"
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Andrew
    family-names: Chang
    affiliation: "Emory University, Department of Computer Science"
    email: andrew.chang@emory.edu
repository-code: "https://github.com/anderooc/llm-refusal-signal"
abstract: >-
  Safety-aligned large language models routinely refuse restricted requests and
  explain why, in the name of transparency and user trust. This work shows that
  those refusal explanations act as an unintended side channel, permitting
  partial reconstruction of hidden system policies without prompt injection or
  jailbreaking. It introduces an evaluation framework for refusal signal
  leakage combining keyword-based detection with embedding similarity between
  refusal explanations and hidden rule descriptions, and demonstrates that the
  leaked signal is sufficient for a separate model instance to reconstruct the
  intent and structure of policies it never observed.
keywords:
  - large language models
  - AI safety
  - semantic leakage
  - prompt injection
  - red teaming
  - refusal behaviour
license: MIT
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2025-12-14"
